{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"dreary-antiutopic-limpet","creation_channel":"python","description":"## The defect\n\n`CodeHealthConfig.enabled` (`src/rebar/_config_schema.py:538`, default `False`) has ZERO read\nsites in `src/rebar`. Nothing gates on it.\n\nA code trace of the live path confirms it: `_commands/metrics.py:139-148` loads\n`config.load_config(root).code_health` and carries only `scan_roots` (:144), `size_cap` (:145) and\n`size_near_fraction` (:146) into the metrics context. `enabled` is never consulted, so the\nstructural metrics (`module_size_distribution`, `oversized_module_count`, `complexity_summary`,\n`duplication_summary`) evaluate even at the default `enabled=False`.\n\nThe field is documented and settable, so an operator can set `enabled = false` and reasonably\nbelieve code-health analysis is off. It is not.\n\n## Why it was never caught\n\nThe 6754 inert-config gate cannot see this one -- see the sibling bug on that gate. It matches\nbare attribute NAMES globally, and `.enabled` is read on other configs (`ui.enabled`,\n`LangfuseConfig`, under `llm/`), so this field passes without ever being read itself. That is also\nwhy it carries no `# read-via:` marker while the neighbouring `analyzers` field needed one.\n\n## The decision this needs\n\nSame shape as dce2: either HONOR the flag (gate structural metric evaluation on it) or DELETE it.\nHonoring it is a behavioral change -- metrics that run today would stop running for anyone on the\ndefault -- so this needs an explicit operator ruling, not an agent's judgment.\n\nNote the interaction with dce2's ruling: the operator chose to DELETE the inert `analyzers` field\nrather than wire it, with intent preserved as idea 94bc-a0b4-bc37-47b2. A consistent ruling here\nis plausible but must not be assumed, because the two differ -- deleting `analyzers` removes a knob\nthat never did anything, whereas deleting `enabled` removes the only advertised off switch for the\nwhole code-health subsystem.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An explicit operator decision is recorded: honor `enabled` or remove it.\n- [ ] If HONORED: structural metric evaluation is gated on it, with a test proving the metrics do\n      NOT evaluate at `enabled=False` and DO at `enabled=True`; the default's behavior change is\n      called out in the changelog.\n- [ ] If REMOVED: the field, its coercion row and its config-surface entries go, and the docs no\n      longer advertise an off switch that does not exist.\n- [ ] Either way, a guard prevents it silently regressing to inert again (this depends on the 6754\n      gate fix -- a same-named field cannot be policed until that lands).\n\n## Provenance\n\nFound by a code-trace archaeology pass over the metrics/code-health system while scoping dce2.\n","id":"a573-00ea-2bf6-4eb1","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"CodeHealthConfig.enabled is inert: structural metrics run regardless of it"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785897856573366001,"uuid":"3ff8b5a0-ff1a-4369-a4c3-f5b77a12f482"}